# NOVA source files are BYTE-SENSITIVE.
#
# A triple-backtick raw text block (```) preserves the source bytes VERBATIM -- that is the
# whole point of the feature (embedded SQL / HTML / JSON / regex with no escape processing).
# So an end-of-line conversion does not merely reformat the file, it CHANGES PROGRAM
# SEMANTICS: the string a text block evaluates to gains or loses \r depending on how the
# repository happened to be checked out.
#
# That is not hypothetical. textblock_test failed ONLY on GitHub's windows-latest runner while
# passing locally, because Windows checks out with core.autocrlf=true. The same file measured
# 31 CR bytes in the local working tree and 69 in a fresh clone -- different bytes, so
# assert_eq against chr(10) held in one place and failed in the other. Nothing was wrong with
# the compiler or the test.
#
# Pinning .nova to LF makes a raw text block evaluate to the same string on every platform and
# every clone, which is the only way that language feature can be sound.
*.nova text eol=lf

# Test fixtures read byte-for-byte by KATs must not be rewritten either.
*.kat  text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. A CRLF bash script fails outright on Linux and macOS with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" -- the whole job dies before running a single test, and the
# error names the interpreter rather than the line endings, which is a genuinely confusing
# hour to lose. Nothing pinned this before; posix_full_suite.sh was one autocrlf away from it.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
